class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
       List< List <Integer> > list1 = new ArrayList<>();
        
          int sum = 0;
        for(int i = 0; i <nums.length; i++){
        if( i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i-1]) continue;
        int l = i + 1, r = nums.length -1;
        while (l < r){
            sum = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r];
            if(sum == 0){
            list1.add(Arrays.asList(nums[i],nums[l],nums[r]));
            l++;
            r--;

            while(l < r && nums[l] == nums[l-1]) l++;
            while(l < r && nums[r] == nums[r+1]) r--;
           }
           else if(sum < 0) l++;
           else r--;


      
         
        }
 
       }
           return list1;
  
    }
}

/*
======================================================================
DESCRIPTION:
This code finds all unique triplets in an array that sum to zero. 
It first sorts the array, then iterates through it, fixing one element 
at a time. For the remaining part of the array, it uses a two-pointer 
approach (left and right) to find pairs that sum up to the negative 
value of the fixed element. It includes logic to skip identical adjacent 
elements to guarantee that the output contains no duplicate triplets.

COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS:
- Time Complexity: $O(n^2)$
  Sorting the array takes $O(n \log n)$ time. The outer `for` loop runs 
  $n$ times. Inside this loop, the `while` loop (two-pointer search) 
  takes $O(n)$ time because the pointers `l` and `r` traverse the 
  remaining array at most once per `i`. Thus, the overall time 
  complexity is $O(n \log n + n^2)$, which simplifies to $O(n^2)$.

- Space Complexity: $O(1)$ or $O(\log n)$
  The auxiliary space complexity (excluding the output list used to 
  store the final triplets) depends on the sorting algorithm used by 
  the language. In Java, Arrays.sort() for primitives uses a Dual-Pivot 
  Quicksort which requires $O(\log n)$ space for the recursion stack. 
  Otherwise, only a few primitive variables are used, making the additional 
  space footprint minimal.
